SPANISH 18
&
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TObjectok0 (* not-passive, not-ergative or ergative subgrammar
                               switch cases e.g. futuro *)
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             subjrel/T1,
             mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu4],
                      mu5
                    ],
             mu6
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             subjrel/T1,
             mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu4],
                      mu5
                    ],
             mu6
           ]
>


      COMP
         <
          C1: (CLAUSErec1.voice <> Passive)   AND
              ((NOT CLAUSErec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012, vp021]) OR
               ((CLAUSErec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012, vp021]) AND
                (T1.CAT IN [NP, NPVAR, CNVAR]) (* special cases e.g. futuro *)
               )
              )
          A1: @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: (CLAUSErec1.voice <> Passive)   AND
              ((NOT CLAUSErec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012, vp021]) OR
               ((CLAUSErec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012, vp021]) AND
                (T1.CAT IN [NP, NPVAR, CNVAR]) (* special cases e.g. futuro *)
               )
              )
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TObjectok0
\item[* relevant examples:]
x1 camina $\rightarrow$ x1 camina\\
redelloNP hay bueyes $\rightarrow$ redelloNP hay bueyes
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] TC:ObjectOkrules
\item[* date of creation:] 11-MAR-1988 10:00:00  
\item[* author:] Elly van Munster
\item[* copied from:] Jan Odijk
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 10/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Added condition for subgrammar switch cases e.g. futuroverb. In the special 
proposition substitution rules these (ergative) verbs already got a subject;
other ergative verbs get a subject in the other objectok rules. Note that it is
also possible to adapt the special proposition substitution rule in such a way 
that it looks like a normal ergative verb at this place!
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To let clauses that contain a subject, and no passive or
ergative verbs  pass this TC.\\
(NB. Also for existentials !! )
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
The following ObjectOK-rules have been made:
\begin{itemize}
\item TobjectOK0:\\

defaultrule (VERB = not-passive and not-ergative).\\
(NB. Also for existentials !!)

\item TobjectOK1a:\\

VERB = passive and not-ergative.\\
object = definite.\\
object becomes subject.

\item TobjectOK1b:\\

VERB = passive and not-ergative.\\
object = non-definite.\\
object becomes subject.

\item TobjectOK2a:\\

VERB = active and ergative.\\
object = definite.\\
object becomes subject.

\item TobjectOK2b:\\

VERB = active and ergative.\\
object = non-definite.\\
object becomes subject.

\item TobjectOK2c:\\

VERB = active and ergative.\\
object = SE.\\
object becomes subject.

\item TobjectOK3a:\\

VERB = passive and not-ergative.\\
object = definite.\\
object remains object.

\item TobjectOK3b:\\

VERB = passive and not-ergative.\\
object = not-definite.\\
object remains object.

\item TobjectOK4a:\\

VERB = not-passive and ergative (vp010 !).\\
object = definite.\\
object remains object.

\item TobjectOK4b:\\

VERB = not-passive and ergative (vp010 !).\\
object = non-definite.\\
object remains object.

\item TobjectOK5:\\

VERB = SEpassive and vp120/vp123.\\
object remains object.\\

\item TobjectOK6a:\\

VERB = passive or ergative.\\
"subject-to-subject raising".\\
complrel/SENTENCE.

\item TobjectOK6b:\\

VERB = passive or ergative.\\
"subject-to-subject raising".\\
complrel/PREPP..[SENTENCE].

\item TobjectOK7:\\

VERB = ergative.\\
vp021 .\\
introduction redello.\\
\end{itemize}

NB. I made the splitting up into rules for {\em def} vs. {\em non-def} 
objects, in order to be able to give a different bonus to these rules.\\
In principle, both def and non-def objects can be either in subject or
in object position in Spanish, but the degree of acceptability is different.\\
e.g. (1) is more natural than (2) although both are correct:\\
(1) - Llegaron veinte soldados. \\
(2) - Veinte soldados llegaron. \\
Note that not only the (non)definiteness of the object is a determining factor,
but also the type of verb (llegar = a "presentational" verb)
\end{description}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TObjectok1a (* passive, not-ergative, object -> subject *)
< m1:I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I3:mu1,
             I4:mu8,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu6,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                      cliticrel/CLITIC{}[mu5],
                      mu7,
                      I2:objrel/T1,                      
                      mu3
                    ],
             mu4
           ]
>

< m :I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I3:mu1,
             I2:subjrel/T1,
             I4:mu8,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu6,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu2],
                      cliticrel/CLITIC{}[mu5],
                      mu7,
                      mu3
                    ],
             mu4
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: (CLAUSErec1.voice = Passive) AND
           NOT (CLAUSErec1.thetavp IN [vp010,vp012, vp021])
       I2: T1.CAT IN [NPVAR,CNVAR,NP]
       I3: ALL (mu1, I31:rel1/..)
       I31: rel1 IN AUX_presubjrels
       I4: NOT EXIST (mu8, [subjrel/..]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu8, [I41:rel2/..])
       I41: rel2 IN AUX_presubjrels
      >


<
   SUBRULE
      < T1: NP{NPrec1}[mu9]>
      < T1: NP{NPrec1}[mu9]>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.definite = def
          A1: @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.definite = def
          A1: @
         >


   SUBRULE
      < T1: NPVAR{NPVARrec1}>
      < T1: NPVAR{NPVARrec1}>


      COMP
         <
          C1: NPVARrec1.definite = def
          A1: @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPVARrec1.definite = def
          A1: @
         >


   SUBRULE
      < T1: CNVAR{CNVARrec1}>
      < T1: CNVAR{CNVARrec1}>


      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
