\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TObjectok7
\item[* relevant examples:]
gustar [x1 nadar] a x1 $\rightarrow$ $<$Redello$>$  $<$Redello$>$ gusta [x1
nadar] a x1\\
Hay bueyes. ????
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] TC:ObjectOkrules
\item[* date of creation:] 11-JAN-1989 
\item[* author:] Lisette Appelo
\item[* copied from:] Tobjectok5
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] 
To introduce in an ergative structure that contains a `subject' 
sentence the dummy `reduced ello' in subject position for subject-verb-
agreement. These verbs only allow for third person singular in these cases.
Subject-to-subject-raising is not possible. In those cases apply 
Tobjectok6(a+b).
`Reduced ello' is deleted later. (see also impersonal verbs such as `llover')\\
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
Two subrules for NPVAR vs. CNVAR.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
